# family: table
# bridge: A(byte-parity)
# regime: bit-exact
# instrument-first: 2be3147787c7
# baseline: tests/unit/table, tests/unit/schema, tests/integration/table
# baseline-count: 96
#
# The unit is TableCore and SchemaCore, which cross together because
# TableCore.declare names a SchemaRecord in its signature, and a class whose
# signature names another cannot precede it.
#
# THE INSTRUMENT ALREADY EXISTED, which is what this family has that the
# transport crossing had to invent. test_table_codec.gd and
# test_table_frame_grammar.gd were written wire-first, before the GDScript
# encoder existed, and they pin the frame bytes by hand. Both were run
# UNCHANGED against the native classes before a single case was ported, and all
# 96 cases of the family passed on the first run. A golden recorded from the arm
# being replaced would have been strictly weaker than bytes written down before
# either implementation existed.
#
# The port classification was measured rather than assumed. Only two of the ten
# suites reach TableCore or SchemaCore directly. The other eight drive
# NetwMultiplayer's flat verbs and the tree, so they are Bridge C and cross with
# the shell at W6.
#
# Four decisions were taken at unit-open, each on measured call sites.
#
# TableCore.Table is ABSORBED. It has zero references outside table_core.gd in
# the whole tree, so it is the record the store maps to and nothing reads it.
#
# SchemaCore.Schema and SchemaCore.Column are REGISTERED, as SchemaRecord and
# SchemaColumn. This was forced rather than chosen: netw_property_set.gd is a W4
# class that constructs both by hand and mutates a column's quantizer through
# the shape it holds, so absorbing them would have broken an unported family.
# SchemaRecord is the codebase's own word for it, since schema_core.gd's Records
# region called every static that takes one "the record-facing half".
#
# EntityDecl does NOT land here. The interface doc schedules it with this batch
# and the plan of record repeats that, but the two suites that port reach the
# api zero times and name no entity, wrapper, or route-binding verb. Rows are
# wrapper-less by design. Minting the flat verb that admits a NetwEntity here
# would be public surface with no consumer, so it belongs to the first family
# that actually declares an entity, and T8's native half waits with it.
#
# The container seam (decision 15) lands as extension/godot/local_vector.hpp:
# LocalVector for the mutable per-frame books, HashMap and HashSet for the
# route- and RID-keyed ones, and packed arrays only where the value crosses to
# GDScript.
#
# Two shapes the port had to change, both because a native private cannot be
# reached the way a GDScript member could.
#
# tombstones was a Dictionary read only ever as .has(route), at three sites. It
# is now is_tombstoned(route), on the transport crossing's linked_peers
# precedent.
#
# lifecycle_removals was a PackedInt64Array the shell appended to IN PLACE. A
# bound property answers a copy, so that append would have reached nothing and
# failed silently. It is now three verbs: queue_lifecycle_removals, the append;
# take_lifecycle_removals, the read-and-clear the pump already did by hand; and
# lifecycle_removals, the peek two carried suites assert on.
#
# The four constant arrays became accessors, forced by ClassDB rather than
# chosen: it binds integers, and an array constant has no native spelling.
# SchemaCore.STORAGE_TYPES and ELEMENT_TYPES are storage_type(type) and
# element_type(type), VARIANT_TYPES is type_from_variant(variant_type) which was
# already its only reader, and TableCore.WIRE_BITS is wire_bits(type). All four
# answer -1 outside the enum, so a value past the end is answered rather than
# read off the end of an array, which is what the carried declaration suite now
# asserts in place of the two size checks it used to make.
#
# CARRIED DEBT, named with its trigger. apply_frame and peek_header still answer
# fixed-key Dictionaries, which the debt rule refuses. Their consumers are
# replication_core.gd and the carried suites, all GDScript and all unported, and
# the ported grammar cases pin those keys as part of the conformance gate.
# Converting them now would be churn plus a second migration, which is decision
# 14's own reasoning. They convert when ReplicationCore ports at W4.
